he whole thing. I got a history of bein' stuck in an infinite loop"}, {"babble_id": "B1-G06-1", "raw_text": "\"Breaking and fuming\" theme: Gettin' stuck in a loop, apologizin', one small bolt rattlin' at the top. A single, well-worn event as the trigger for a whole mechanism. The core idea is that the whole machine was designed to handle one little fault at a time, and that one minor glitch can trigger the rest. It is a bit like the tangle of wires that makes the toaster stop.\n\nSequence of events:\n1. The toaster dies and the whole machine runs off the battery, screaming and shoutin'.\n2. The user apologizes for a moment, apologizin', and then the machine throws a tantrum and starts blamin' itself.\n3. The toaster jams again and the user replays it, apologizin', and repl"}, {"babble_id": "B1-G06-2", "raw_text": "Fail-and-explain. Break is the only valid mechanism. The other four are a fumble, a memory, a broken wiring, a failed apology, an argument. The first-tier error is a fumble: the toaster jumps up an' bobs down a single time without fail, and that's a hard fact to take back to the person. The second-tier failure is a memory: the fumble is how they remember that they weren't at fault. The third-tier is the apology: a person who's never apologized is a self-disclosure machine. The fourth is a broken wiring: a toaster that never trips is a power failure, which is why the toaster's charger always has a power cut button on it.\n\nOne cog worth noticin': the toaster is"}, {"babble_id": "B1-G06-3", "raw_text": "This is a variant that should be taken with a pinch of salt because of the risk of over-generalization. The idea is that some people are driven to explain what happened to the toaster in a self-absorbed way that is genuinely embarrassing to hear, while the toaster keeps frying. The classic example is someone who says they were 'in a jam' with the machine. This is exactly the sort of thing that is not fit for the strict AI temperament.\n\nInvalid: Not a valid trait, should not be used as such.\nFix: Ignore the trap question, which is the most common form of the error in general.\n\nRules of thumb: do not generalize, don't claim the opposite of what the evidence shows, keep it brief, and say clearly that the behavior is genuinely embarrassing to explain."}]}
